is crucial that the EU does not supply  or help the goods or services that could  lead to the death penalty or torture  Europe's lead in technological  innovation can be a double-edged sword  when it comes to tools and services  exported to countries which torture and  kill their citizens human rights  organizations say torture continues in  half of the hundred and fifty eight  countries that ratified a convention  banning it and the global trade in tools  used for it is booming the space for  civil society the democratic countries  in the world are shrinking we've just  seen that press freedom worldwide is  also diminishing and so we as  representatives in open societies and  democracies have to be very vigilant EU  law binding on all Member States was  agreed in 2005 but despite changes since  many loopholes remain now Parliament  urged by civil society bodies is seeking  to tighten those we are seeking to limit  the export of very blatant tools that  are used for for torture such as  changing people or beating people but  also medicines that could be used to  give a lethal injection for example  should not be sold to prisons or law  enforcement agencies there is evidence  that existing legislation works it's  been reported to parliament from the USA  that the ban on medical substances that  can be used for executions there has  made them more difficult to perform
 

